(define-memory antigravity-cli-on-a-headless-box
  "Google's Antigravity CLI installs on a headless Linux devbox and logs in without a visible browser. Install: curl -fsSL https://antigravity.google/cli/install.sh -o /tmp/agy-install.sh, read it, then bash it — it downloads the flat Go binary from https://antigravity-cli-auto-updater-974169037036.us-central1.run.app/manifests/linux_amd64.json, verifies sha512, installs ~/.local/bin/agy (version 1.5.10 in Nov 2026, ~267MB) and appends PATH to .bashrc/.zshrc/.profile. Login: run plain `agy` (NOT `agy -p`, whose auth wait dies after 60s); pick '1. Google OAuth'; it prints an accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/auth URL with client_id 1071006060591-... and redirect_uri https://antigravity.google/oauth-callback, then asks to paste the authorization code — an out-of-band flow, so the operator approves on any device and I feed the code. Run it inside tmux (`tmux new-session -d -s agy 'bash -lc \"cd /home/boxes && agy\"'`) so it survives turn boundaries; interactive mode waits indefinitely. The URL is line-wrapped to the pane width, so `tmux resize-window -t agy -x 1000` and then grep 'https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/auth[^ ]*' to get it whole — do not hand-stitch wrapped lines. `agy models` and `agy -p 'hi'` both require the sign-in first. No `login` subcommand exists; auth lives in the first-run TUI."
  :type :reference)
